## Mutations Pattern

Mutations are called directly in components/handlers — no wrapper hook needed unless the mutation is reused in 2+ places.

```typescript
import { useMutation } from '@apollo/client'
import { graphql } from '../lib/tada'
import { showSuccess, showError } from '../lib/toast'

const INSERT_TALE = graphql(`
  mutation InsertTale($object: tales_insert_input!) {
    insert_tales_one(object: $object) {
      id
      created_at
    }
  }
`)

// In component:
const [insertTale, { loading }] = useMutation(INSERT_TALE, {
  onCompleted: () => {
    showSuccess('Your tale has been saved ✨')
    router.replace('/(app)/')
  },
  onError: (error) => {
    console.error('[InsertTale]', error)
    showError('Something went wrong — please try again')
  },
  // Refetch the feed so the new tale appears immediately
  refetchQueries: ['GetTalesFeed'],
})
```

---

## Nested Inserts

Use Hasura's nested insert syntax to insert a tale and all related records in a single mutation. Never chain multiple mutations sequentially.

```graphql
mutation InsertTale($object: tales_insert_input!) {
  insert_tales_one(object: $object) {
    id
    tale_children { child { id name } }
    tale_tags { tag }
    tale_media { url }
  }
}
```

Variables:
```typescript
{
  object: {
    narrative: "...",
    title: "...",
    family_account_id: currentUser.familyAccountId,
    author_id: currentUser.id,
    tale_children: {
      data: selectedChildIds.map(id => ({ child_id: id }))
    },
    tale_tags: {
      data: tags.map(tag => ({ tag }))
    },
    tale_media: {
      data: mediaUrls.map((url, i) => ({ url, media_type: 'photo', display_order: i }))
    }
  }
}
```

---

## Subscriptions

Use `useSubscription` from Apollo for real-time data. Subscriptions connect via WebSocket to `EXPO_PUBLIC_HASURA_WS_ENDPOINT`.

```typescript
import { useSubscription } from '@apollo/client'
import { graphql } from '../lib/tada'

const ON_TALE_COMMENTS = graphql(`
  subscription OnTaleComments($taleId: uuid!) {
    comments(
      where: { tale_id: { _eq: $taleId } }
      order_by: { created_at: asc }
    ) {
      id
      content
      created_at
      user { id name avatar_url }
    }
  }
`)

const { data } = useSubscription(ON_TALE_COMMENTS, {
  variables: { taleId },
})
```

Only use subscriptions for genuinely real-time data (comments, reactions). Use queries + `refetchQueries` for everything else.

---

## Hasura Variable Conventions

| Hasura type | TypeScript type | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| `uuid!` | `string` | All IDs |
| `timestamptz` | `string` (ISO 8601) | Dates — use `new Date().toISOString()` |
| `_eq`, `_lt`, `_gt` | Comparison operators | Filter variables |
| `order_by` | `{ field: 'asc' \| 'desc' }` | Sorting |

---

## Apollo Cache

- After a mutation that adds a new item to a list, use `refetchQueries: ['GetTalesFeed']` rather than manual cache updates — simpler and less error-prone for v1
- After a delete mutation, use `refetchQueries` for the relevant list query
- Do not use `cache.modify()` unless there is a measurable performance reason

---

## Hasura Actions

Hasura Actions (e.g. `processAudioTale`, `acceptGuestInvite`) are called like regular mutations but trigger a Cloudflare Worker instead of a direct DB write.

```graphql
mutation ProcessAudioTale($audioUrl: String!, $childIds: [uuid!]!, $childNames: [String!]!) {
  processAudioTale(audio_url: $audioUrl, child_ids: $childIds, child_names: $childNames) {
    narrative
    title
    tags
  }
}
```

Actions are defined in `hasura/metadata/actions.yaml` — do not modify the mobile-side query pattern for actions vs regular mutations.
